-- title:
--   Hecke operators killing inertia displacements kill the Eisenstein Tate module
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime, and let $B$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbf Q} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ` lying over $2$, in the sense that the image of $2$ lies in the non-units of $B$. Write $J =$ `JZero p` for the degree-zero divisor class group $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the level-$p$ modular function field over $\overline{\mathbf Q}$, equipped with its module structure over $\mathbb T =$ `HeckeAlg` $= \mathbf Z[\,T_\ell : \ell \text{ prime}\,]$ given by `heckeModuleBar p`, and let $T =$ [`TateModule 2 (JZero p)`](def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L15) be the group of sequences $x : \mathbf N \to J$ with $2^n x_n = 0$ and $2x_{n+1} = x_n$. Let $\alpha$ be any element of the $\mathbf Z_2$-subalgebra of $\mathrm{End}_{\mathbf Z_2}(T)$ generated by the termwise actions `tateHeckeRep 2 (JZero p) t` of the elements $t \in \mathbb T$. For $M \in \mathbf N$ let `eisensteinTorsionBar p 2 M` be the subgroup of elements of $J$ annihilated by the $M$-th power of the Eisenstein ideal, namely the preimage under `eisensteinEval p` of the ideal $(2) \subseteq \mathbf Z$, and let $D \subseteq J$ be the subgroup generated by all $\sigma x - x$ with $\sigma$ in the image in $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbf Q}/\mathbf Q)$ of the inertia subgroup of $B$ over $\mathbf Q$ and $x$ annihilated by some power of the Eisenstein ideal. The assertion is: if $\alpha s = 0$ for every $s \in T$ all of whose components $s_n$ lie in $D$, then $\alpha x = 0$ for every $x \in T$ such that each component $x_n$ is annihilated by some power (depending on $n$) of the Eisenstein ideal.
--
--   This is the faithfulness statement for the inertia-displacement part of the $2$-adic Tate module of $J_0(p)$ as a module over the $\mathbf Z_2$-algebra generated by the Hecke operators: an element of that algebra vanishing on the displacement sublattice already vanishes on the whole Eisenstein part. It is used in the construction of a generator of the intersection of the Tate module with the displacement and Eisenstein-torsion conditions.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_tateHeckeRep_eq_zero_of_forall_closure_inertia_smul_sub_eq_zero.lean

